    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` feature for `POST /api/owners`, keeping to the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**Behavior:** When a create request sets `sharesHousehold: true`, the existing `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` step already skips its duplicate-rejection (so an owner at an address already used by a same-lastName owner is allowed). I added assignment of a shared, stable `householdId` and made it available on the owner response.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerHousehold.java`** (new function) \u2014 runs after `BuildOwner`. When `sharesHousehold` is true, it derives a deterministic `householdId` (first 16 upper-hex chars of SHA-256 over the canonical last name + address \u2014 same case-insensitive/whitespace-collapsed canonicalization the uniqueness check uses), sets it on the new owner, and back-fills the same id onto existing household members (\"assign both the same householdId\"). Because it's derived from last name + address, it's stable across requests. Owners that don't opt in keep a null `householdId`.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `assignHousehold` step between `build` and `assignCustomerCode`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `household_id` column field with getter/setter.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it into GET responses automatically.\n\n5. **`schema.sql`** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `household_id` column. Tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema file is what matters.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` and the full build both pass.",
